  "I_New" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Given RPE promising status and practical limitations, therefore RPE status is promising but flawed."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to develop and evaluate a drift-resilient quantum metrology protocol using shallow-depth circuits, aimed at removing the phase-matching constraint in phase estimation and achieving Heisenberg-scaling, Cramér-Rao-optimal calibration of multi-parameter two-qubit gate phases with target precision around 10^-4 radians.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing calibration approaches face depth, drift, scaling, and CRB-optimality limitations, and phase matching is a critical condition, the scientific response is to develop and evaluate a protocol designed to overcome those limits."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The two prevalent calibration approaches considered here, RPE and randomized benchmarking, each have important limitations relative to short-depth, Heisenberg-scaling, drift-robust metrology."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) For CRB-based assessment, optimal inference subroutines are relevant because meeting classical Cramér-Rao bounds requires such subroutine optimality."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Achieving quantum Cramér-Rao bounds requires optimal quantum measurement schemes and refined techniques for realizing that optimality."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) CRB-oriented metrology design therefore involves both classically optimal inference subroutines and quantum measurement/refinement requirements."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The QEC-driven need for improved metrology and the limitations of RPE and randomized benchmarking motivate investigating a new quantum metrology protocol."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) A new RPE-like metrology design should address phase-matching constraints together with classical inference and quantum measurement/refinement requirements."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) These motivations and design requirements support developing and evaluating a new robust, shallow quantum metrology protocol."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Coefficient-optimality challenges and severe practical hurdles are both relevant barriers for quantum metrology strategies."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The QEC accuracy gap, together with coefficient and practical barriers, motivates improved quantum metrology rather than proving a specific solution."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) RPE remains promising but practically limited for short-depth, drift-affected use, especially because recent refinements focus on asymptotic rather than short-depth regimes."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) If efficiency is influenced by Heisenberg limit and coefficients, then coefficient optimality is fundamental."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Pattern RPE precision drops to 10^-1 and 10^-2 radians with time-dependent drifts."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Pattern recent progress refines RPE cost but focuses on asymptotic regime rather than short-depth."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Instance randomized benchmarking approach is general but forgoes Heisenberg scaling."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Pattern RB requires extensive depth and lacks sensitivity to coherent rotation errors."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) If effectiveness is assessed by CRB, then classical optimality is required for effectiveness."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently inference subroutines must be optimal to meet classical Cramér-Rao bounds."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently quantum measurement schemes must be optimal to achieve quantum Cramér-Rao bounds."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) If realizing optimality requires refining techniques, then quantum optimality is required for effectiveness."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) If diagonal elements must share phase, then phase-matching is a critical condition for RPE success."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently strategies adhere to Heisenberg scaling, but challenge lies in scaling coefficients."];
  "S20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently violation of phase-matching leads to failure in achieving Heisenberg scaling and CRB."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) If QEC demands high accuracy, then fault-tolerant thresholds require exceptional gate characterization."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently lack of optimality in scaling coefficients necessitates deep circuitry."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Pattern inefficiency is compounded by finite coherence times and drift errors."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently quantum metrology protocols fall short of accuracy needed for fault-tolerant threshold."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently RPE and randomized benchmarking are two primary approaches for gate calibration."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) If RPE achieves Heisenberg limit ideally, then RPE is a promising primary approach."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) Instance RPE implementation is limited by need for deep circuits and optimizations."];
